Both forms side by side, verified:

```tsx
// CONTROLLED — React state is the single source of truth
const [email, setEmail] = useState('');
<input value={email} onChange={(e) => setEmail(e.target.value)} />
// every keystroke: onChange → setState → re-render → input displays state
// instant validation, format-on-type, disable-until-valid all for free

// UNCONTROLLED — the DOM owns the value; read it when you need it
const emailRef = useRef<HTMLInputElement>(null);
<form onSubmit={(e) => {
  e.preventDefault();
  const value = emailRef.current!.value;   // read at the boundary
  submit(value);
}}>
  <input defaultValue="" ref={emailRef} />
</form>
```

The control loop (the interview core): controlled inputs make the data flow a CIRCLE — user types → onChange fires → you setState → React re-renders → the input's value attribute is what state now says. This is why you can transform as the user types (uppercase, mask, strip): the input never shows anything React didn't approve. It's also the cost: every keystroke re-renders the component (fine for forms; the scaling concern only matters for very large inputs or high-frequency values like sliders/drag — debounce the heavy work, not the state).

Uncontrolled economics: no re-renders per keystroke, no state plumbing — the DOM keeps its own value between renders (same as plain HTML). The ref is the bridge: read it at boundaries (submit, blur, a save), never to drive render. defaultValue (not value) sets the initial content once; file inputs are uncontrolled ONLY (browser security owns the FileList — you can't set value).

The decision rule (say it unprompted): controlled by default — you want validation and a live form model in React. Uncontrolled when (a) the form is huge and keystroke re-renders measurably hurt, (b) you integrate a non-React widget that must own its DOM value (a rich text editor, a map search box), or (c) it's a file input. Mixed forms are fine — one form can have controlled fields with validation logic and uncontrolled long-text fields; just never let a single input flip between modes.
